e":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Beirut","confidence":0.8}],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":68,"confidence":0.6},"profileConfidence":0.78,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"West Beirut","confidence":0.55},{"name":"Sabra and Shatila","confidence":0.45}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"31f0443eb6298b3b8505b9f162db3e723b8ed815f4c6eb5dd44caf5b53fd34c5","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"How the Palestine Liberation Organization Research Center informed the PLO’s relationship to Zionism and Israel In September 1982, the Israeli military invaded West Beirut and Israel-allied Lebanese militiamen massacred Palestinians in the Sabra and Shatila refugee camps. Meanwhile, Israeli forces also raided the Palestine Liberation Organization Research Center and trucked its complete library to Israel. Palestinian activists and supporters protested loudly to international organizations and the Western press, claiming that the assault on the Center proved that the Israelis sought to destroy not merely Palestinian militants but Palestinian culture as well. The protests succeeded: in November 1983, Israel returned the library as part of a prisoner exchange. What was in that library? Much of the expansive collection the PLO amassed consisted of books about Judaism, Zionism, and Israel. In Reading Herzl in Beirut, Jonathan Marc Gribetz tells the story of the PLO Research Center from its establishment in 1965 until its ultimate expulsion from Lebanon in 1983. Gribetz explores why the PLO invested in research about the Jews, what its researchers learned about Judaism and Zionism, and how the knowledge they acquired informed the PLO’s relationship to Israel.","pageCount":408,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-reading-herzl-in-beirut-the-plo-effort-to-know-the-enemy-jonathan-marc-gribetz"}],"displaySummary":"The protests succeeded: in November 1983, Israel returned the library as part of a prisoner exchange. What was in that library? Much of the expansive collection the PLO amassed consisted of books about Judaism, Zionism, and Israel. In Reading Herzl in Beirut, Jonathan Marc Gribetz tells the story of the PLO Research Center from its establishment in 1965 until its ultimate expulsion from Lebanon in 1983. Gribetz explores why the PLO invested in research about the Jews, what its researchers learned about Judaism and Zionism, and how the knowledge they acquired informed the PLO’s relationship to Israel.","primarySubject":"History","primaryTopic":"Immigration, Refugees & Borderlands","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"History","score":3,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":3,"confidence":"medium","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-reading-herzl-in-beirut-the-plo-effort-to-know-the-enemy-jonathan-marc-gribetz","id":"subject-evidence-book-reading-herzl-in-beirut-the-plo-effort-to-know-the-enemy-jonathan-marc-gribetz-1"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"Race & Ethnicity","mappedSubject":"Race & Ethnicity","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"history keyword or history prize; war/military keyword; society/culture keyword; religion keyword; race/ethnicity keyword","id":"subject-evidence-book-reading-herzl-in-beirut-the-plo-effort-to-know-the-enemy-jonathan-marc-gr